# Regulatory & Political Brief — Quiet-by-Design at IAD and DCA

*Independent Stage 1 brief. Written without reading peer briefs. Focus: what binds an MWAA quiet-by-design standard, what looks like a design choice but is actually federal law, and what can and cannot be moved on the operator's decision calendar.*

---

## Key findings (lead each with the operative fact)

1. **Every PA silence target lives underneath a hard floor.** NFPA 72, Chapter 24 (adopted through the Virginia and D.C. building codes) requires that emergency voice communications produce a Speech Transmission Index (STI) of at least 0.45 at 90 percent of measurement locations in every acoustically distinguishable space (ADS), with an average of 0.50 [Source: https://www.nfpa.org/codes-and-standards/nfpa-72-standard-development/72]. A "quiet" spec that does not begin with the ADS survey and the STI target is not a standard; it is a wish.

2. **The 2024 DOT wheelchair rule and 14 CFR Part 382 Subpart D make redundant communication a federal obligation, not a design preference.** Part 382 already requires accessible visual paging, TDDs, and effective communication at airports covered by 49 U.S.C. 47107 [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-II/subchapter-D/part-382/subpart-D]. The DOT final rule effective January 16, 2025 layered on prompt-assistance and training obligations that presume airline-airport coordination on real-time information [Source: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/12/17/2024-29731/ensuring-safe-accommodations-for-air-travelers-with-disabilities-using-wheelchairs]. Any MWAA standard that reduces audible paging without codifying the visual, digital, and staffed substitutes puts the Authority on the wrong side of Part 382.

3. **The PFC cap has been $4.50 since 2000; the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 held it there over industry objection.** ACI-NA and AAAE lobbied for $8; Congress kept $4.50 [Source: https://reason.org/wp-content/uploads/modernizing-passenger-facility-charge.pdf]. The operative implication for this run: MWAA cannot fund a quiet-by-design retrofit program out of an expanded PFC. The revenue is what the 2024 use agreement priced, and the next chance to move the cap is the FY2029 reauthorization cycle. Design the standard as if PFCs will not grow, because they will not.

4. **DCA's operating envelope is set by statute, not by MWAA.** The 2024 Reauthorization Act (P.L. 118-63, enacted May 16, 2024) added 10 slot exemptions at DCA — five beyond-perimeter round trips — with service required by March 17, 2025 [Source: https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/COMPS-17764/pdf/COMPS-17764.pdf]. That decision was congressional, not operational; it is why DCA now runs transcontinental A321s through a terminal built for regional peaks. The acoustic standard at DCA inherits a passenger profile Congress delivered.

5. **The Saarinen main terminal is a National Register property; any federally-assisted work triggers Section 106 review.** The IAD main terminal was listed May 5, 1978 [Source: https://www.docomomo-us.org/register/washington-dulles-international-airport-main-terminal]. Acoustic treatments, ceiling-mounted loudspeaker arrays, and any visible PA hardware in the head house are Section 106-consulted with the Virginia SHPO whenever AIP, BIL, or FAA discretionary dollars are in the funding stack. Historic preservation is the constraint that most often surprises interior-fitout teams.

6. **The interstate compact that created MWAA is not decorative — it gives Congress a standing seat at the table.** MWAA is the creature of Virginia Code § 5.1-152 and D.C. Code § 9-901, ratified by Congress in 49 U.S.C. § 49106 [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/49106]. The Board can adopt technical standards without a federal vote, but any standard that reshapes the passenger-facing terminal at DCA will be read by the Metropolitan Washington delegation and the House T&I majority as a political act. Plan accordingly.

7. **OSHA and Part 139 set floors from the other direction — employee exposure and operational content.** 29 CFR 1910.95 sets an 85 dBA 8-hour TWA action level and 90 dBA permissible exposure limit for employees at gates and checkpoints [Source: https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.95]. FAA 14 CFR Part 139 keeps operational and safety announcements on the required-content list even where MWAA choreographs their delivery [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-G/part-139]. Silence is bounded on both ends.

8. **The 2025 airline use agreement, not the 2026 program announcement, is the binding commercial envelope for this standard.** The 15-year Airport Use Agreement and Premises Lease approved by the Board in 2024 and effective January 1, 2025 governs airline consent to premises changes through 2039 [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/board/board-actions]. The July 29, 2026 $20B+ Dulles announcement is a program vision; the airline consent mechanics still run through the 2025 use agreement's majority-in-interest and consultation provisions. A quiet-by-design standard adopted before the Fall 2027 CIP amendment window enters the airline conversation on the Authority's terms.

