## Evidence — what binds, and where

### The federal building code that most people call "acoustics" is actually life safety

NFPA 72's Chapter 24 (Emergency Communications Systems) is the load-bearing rule for any PA-related quiet standard. Annex D operationalizes intelligibility as an STI ≥ 0.45 at 90 percent of ADS measurement points and an average of 0.50 [Source: https://www.nationaltrainingcenter.com/voice-intelligibility/]. Virginia has adopted the 2018 IBC/IFC with amendments (13 VAC 5-63); the District has adopted the 2017 D.C. Construction Codes; both incorporate NFPA 72. That is why an "ambient sound target" specification that omits the ADS survey misreads the code — the STI target is enforceable through the AHJ at commissioning and can hold up a certificate of occupancy. Standard-setting begins here or it does not begin.

### The accessibility stack is denser than the design guides suggest

14 CFR Part 382 Subpart D (Accessibility of Airport Facilities) applies to any airport that receives federal funds; MWAA does [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-II/subchapter-D/part-382/subpart-D]. FAA Advisory Circular 150/5360-14A ("Access to Airports by Individuals with Disabilities") is the operational reading of Part 382 and it explicitly discusses visual paging, TDD/TTY, and information redundancy [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/150-5360-14A.pdf]. The 2010 ADA Standards for Accessible Design (28 CFR Part 35) add Section 706 (assistive listening) and Section 810 (transportation facilities) [Source: https://www.access-board.gov/ada/]. The December 17, 2024 DOT final rule (89 FR 102422) does not directly regulate acoustics but imports assumptions about coordinated real-time passenger information that a quiet standard must satisfy [Source: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/12/17/2024-29731/ensuring-safe-accommodations-for-air-travelers-with-disabilities-using-wheelchairs]. A quieter announcement policy therefore cannot be adopted without simultaneously specifying, funding, and commissioning the visual, digital, and staffed substitutes. That is a design-brief line item, not an operating-department problem.

### Historic preservation is the interior-fitout trap

The Saarinen main terminal's National Register listing (May 5, 1978) is not honorary. Whenever federal money touches the property — AIP, BIL, FAA discretionary — Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act requires consultation with the Virginia SHPO on any visible modification [Source: https://www.achp.gov/protecting-historic-properties/section-106-process/introduction-section-106]. That covers loudspeaker arrays, digital signage housings, and acoustic panels in the head house. DCA's Terminal 1 (Bolling, 1941) sits inside the George Washington Memorial Parkway historic district and is subject to similar review; the Pelli-designed Terminal 2 is not historic but its architectural-intent easements limit ceiling and column penetrations. The costliest words a designer can hear at 60-percent pricing are "the SHPO would like to see alternatives." Bake preservation review into the schematic-design phase gate or absorb the schedule slip later.

### The political ceiling on funding

The PFC cap held at $4.50 in the 2024 reauthorization is the single most important political fact for capital planning at MWAA [Source: https://reason.org/wp-content/uploads/modernizing-passenger-facility-charge.pdf]. Every time the industry has pushed to raise it — 2012, 2016, 2018, 2024 — the airline lobby has beaten it back. The next opportunity is the FY2029 reauthorization; assume it holds. AIP formula grants are also fixed by the reauthorization at approximately $3.35B annually [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12791]. BIL supplemental airport funding runs through FY2026 and does not renew automatically. This is why the operative funding question for a quiet standard is *not* "can we raise revenue for it" — it is "can we bake it into the CIP before schematic-design cost estimates lock, so it comes free with the concrete."

### The DCA political envelope

DCA is not a normal airport politically. The perimeter rule (14 CFR 93.253, in force since 1966) and the slot rule (14 CFR Part 93 Subpart K) are administered by the FAA but written and periodically rewritten by Congress. The 2024 addition of five beyond-perimeter round trips (10 daily slot exemptions) is the fifth expansion since 2000 [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12791]. Every expansion is opposed by the Virginia and Maryland delegations and championed by senators from cities without DCA service. The pattern is durable: the perimeter rule is uneconomic, everyone agrees it is uneconomic, and no reauthorization removes it. A quiet-by-design standard at DCA that assumes stable passenger volumes is fighting a political current that has produced a slot-exemption round in four of the last six reauthorization cycles.

### Interstate compact and the "who really approves this" question

49 U.S.C. § 49106 requires that MWAA's bylaws be filed with Congress and gives the Secretary of Transportation certain reserved consultation rights [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/49106]. In practice, technical design manuals are a Board action; nothing about a quiet-by-design standard requires DOT sign-off. But the Metropolitan Washington delegation reads MWAA as a federal instrumentality and every substantial passenger-experience change at DCA has produced constituent mail. A CEO-issued design manual amendment for IAD is technocratic; the same amendment at DCA is a political event even when the text is identical.

