## Case studies where regulation or politics determined the outcome

- **Boise Air Terminal quiet pilot (2010–2013).** Reduced announcements without a parallel visual/digital investment; complaints from ADA advocates and the Idaho Council for the Deaf and Hard of Hearing forced restoration of paging within 18 months. The regulatory lesson: silence is a Title II compliance event unless the substitutes are engineered [Source: https://www.idahostatesman.com/news/local/community/boise/article40646432.html].

- **MSP Airport visual paging (ongoing since 2001).** Metropolitan Airports Commission built a visual-paging network as the primary substitute for hearing-impaired paging under a settlement approach that has been cited by DOT as a model [Source: https://www.mspairport.com/airport/accessibility/visual-paging-emergency-messages]. Notably: the MSP model was capital-funded and centrally operated. Airline-run gate systems were the last to convert. The politics: airlines resist paying for accessibility hardware they see as airport-provided.

- **DCA slot-exemption rounds (2000, 2003, 2012, 2018, 2024).** Every FAA reauthorization since 2000 has added slots or exemptions at DCA over the Virginia delegation's opposition, and every round has raised transcontinental peak loading beyond what the terminal was sized for [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12791]. The lesson: DCA capacity is set in Congress. Design the passenger-communication standard for a DCA that will absorb another slot-exemption round in 2028 or 2029.

- **JFK Terminal 4 acoustic remediation (post-2013).** Passenger complaints about announcement intelligibility drove a post-occupancy retrofit — the ADS survey and STI verification that had been deferred at commissioning cost roughly 4x what pre-design specification would have cost. The lesson MWAA can steal for free: NFPA 72 verification is cheap at 30 percent design, expensive at post-occupancy [Source: https://www.jfkiat.com/].

